.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Eigen (1954) measured reactions far too fast to mix by perturbing an equilibrium mixture (a sudden temperature, pressure, or field jump) and timing its exponential return to the new equilibrium. For A \rightleftharpoons B the relaxation time is \tau = 1/(k_f + k_r), so combining \tau with the equilibrium constant K = k_f/k_r yields both rate constants. For the association A + B \rightleftharpoons C linearizing about equilibrium gives 1/\tau = k_f([A]_{eq} + [B]_{eq}) + k_r, the formula Eigen used to show that H^+ + OH^- recombination is diffusion-controlled.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/kinetics/networks/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_04_eigen_relaxation_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/kinetics/networks/plot_04_eigen_relaxation`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Eigen's chemical relaxation: temperature jump and relaxation time</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/kinetics/networks/plot_04_eigen_relaxation`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Below the Hopf-bifurcation threshold (:math:`B &lt; 1 + A^2`) the Brusselator&#x27;s single fixed point is a stable focus and any initial concentration relaxes toward it. Above threshold, that same fixed point becomes unstable and every trajectory is drawn instead onto a surrounding stable limit cycle -- sustained chemical oscillation with no external forcing, driven purely by the autocatalytic X^2 Y term.">

